# Cloudflare Pages response headers for the MyKurda web app (mykurda.com).
# The app is a self-contained Vite SPA: it loads only its own hashed
# /assets/*.js + /assets/*.css and talks to the Render API over HTTPS. The
# policy below allows exactly that. If the API origin changes (VITE_API_URL),
# update connect-src to match; if the app renders R2/CDN media, img-src/media-src
# already allow https:.
#
# style-src carries 'unsafe-inline' because React writes every inline style as
# a style attribute, and this app has 128 of them across 44 files. Without it
# the browser blocks the attribute — not only <style> blocks — and drops the
# declaration silently: no console error unless something is listening for
# securitypolicyviolation. Live, mykurda.com reported effectiveDirective
# "style-src-attr", blockedURI "inline".
#
# What that cost, for as long as the app has been deployed: the race and
# Wordle-battle progress bars never moved, the XP level bar never filled, and
# the photo editor's colour picker had no gradient.
#
# script-src stays 'self' with no 'unsafe-inline', which is the directive
# that matters: CSS-only exfiltration needs markup injection first, and that
# is what script-src and the escaping in React already prevent.
#
# style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline' would be tighter, but a browser without
# CSP3 attribute support falls back to style-src and silently reproduces
# exactly this bug, so it is not the safer option it looks like.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self'; media-src 'self' https:; connect-src 'self' https://kurda-api.onrender.com; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# The public dictionary (/ferheng/*), which is a different kind of page.
#
# `_headers` rules ADD, they do not replace: a /ferheng/ page matches `/*`
# above as well as this, and is served every header from both. Measured on
# the live site — two Content-Security-Policy headers, and a
# Strict-Transport-Security whose value was its own value twice, comma-joined.
# That last one matters: the preload list wants one well-formed header.
#
# So this block names only what differs, and the shared headers are left to
# `/*`. The second CSP is deliberate and is the exception: a browser given two
# policies enforces BOTH, so the result is their intersection and can only be
# stricter than either. `/*` allows scripts from self; this names no
# script-src at all and falls back to `default-src 'none'`, so the intersection
# forbids them outright. Same for inline style, which `/*` permits and this
# does not.
#
# That is the second of the two things between a stranger's Wiktionary text
# and code running here. The first is the escaping in web/scripts/escape.ts,
# and this one does not depend on that one being right. Verified live: a
# fetch() from one of these pages is blocked before it leaves.
#
# A longer cache than the app, because a word's meaning does not change
# between deploys. Not `immutable`: the next import rewrites it in place.
# `font-src 'self'` is the one thing added since: the typefaces are copied out
# of node_modules into /ferheng/fonts/ at build time and served from this
# origin. A font from a CDN would need a host named here, which would be the
# first third party these pages talk to; a file on the edge costs nothing and
# tells nobody that somebody read a word.
/ferheng/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400
